 {"id": "36c094ca5b33", "title": "Deduplicate Telegram bot subscribe/keyboard logic in telegram_bot.py", "description": "`app/core/telegram_bot.py` has significant duplication:\n\n1. `subscribe_to_endpoint_callback` (lines ~325-409) and `subscribe_to_endpoint` (lines ~411-483) implement almost the same subscribe-or-activate logic against two different Update surfaces (callback query vs message). The only real difference is which reply method is called and how the endpoint is fetched.\n2. `subscribe_command` (~150-196) and `refresh_endpoints_callback` (~525-566) both build an identical inline keyboard from the endpoint list and post the identical message text.\n3. `handler_command` for `/unsubscribe` is currently wired to `list_subscriptions_command` \u2014 probably a copy/paste bug but confirm before changing behavior; if intentional, add a short comment noting so.\n\n## What to change (ONLY this file)\n1. Extract `_upsert_subscription(cur, endpoint_id: int, chat_id: str) -> tuple[str, str]` returning `(status, endpoint_name)` where status is one of `\"already\"`, `\"reactivated\"`, `\"created\"`. Both `subscribe_to_endpoint_callback` and `subscribe_to_endpoint` should call it and pick the right reply text from a small dict.\n2. Extract `_build_endpoint_keyboard(endpoints: list[dict]) -> InlineKeyboardMarkup` and `_ENDPOINT_LIST_TEXT: str` module constants; use them in both `subscribe_command` and `refresh_endpoints_callback`.\n3. For the `/unsubscribe` handler wiring: if it is a bug (users expect unsubscribe, not list), rewire to a new small handler that shows only unsubscribe buttons. If preserving old behavior, add a `# NOTE: /unsubscribe intentionally reuses /list to show the unsubscribe buttons inline.` comment on that line.\n4. Preserve every user-facing message string exactly. No emoji or wording drift.\n5. Do not touch the DB schema, other modules, or add tests (Task 1 owns tests).\n\n## Files you own\n`app/core/telegram_bot.py` \u2014 nothing else.\n\n## Acceptance\n- `grep -c \"_upsert_subscription\" app/core/telegram_bot.py` returns >= 3 (definition + 2 call sites).\n- `grep -c \"_build_endpoint_keyboard\" app/core/telegram_bot.py` returns >= 3.\n- `python -c \"import app.core.telegram_bot\"` exits 0.\n- Net line count of the file drops by at least 80 lines vs before (there is ~180 lines of duplicate code).", "role": "backend", "priority": 0, "scope": "medium", "complexity": "medium", "estimated_minutes": 240, "status": "open", "task_type": "standard", "upgrade_details": null, "depends_on": [], "parent_task_id": null, "depends_on_repo": null, "owned_files": ["app/core/telegram_bot.py"], "assigned_agent": null, "result_summary": null, "tenant_id": "default", "cell_id": null, "repo": null, "batch_eligible": false, "eu_ai_act_risk": "minimal", "approval_required": false, "risk_level": "low", "slack_context": null, "version": 7, "claimed_at": null, "completed_at": null, "closed_at": null, "claimed_by_session": null, "parent_session_id": null, "subtask_wait_started_at": null, "parent_context": null, "retry_count": 1, "max_retries": 3, "retry_delay_s": 0.0, "terminal_reason": null, "max_output_tokens": null, "meta_messages": ["Retry 1: Previous attempt failed with reason: Agent backend-c1a0df4d died; janitor failed: ['file_contains: app/core/telegram_bot.py :: _upsert_subscription (not found)', 'file_contains: app/core/telegram_bot.py :: _build_endpoint_keyboard (not found)', 'test_passes: python -c \"import app.core.telegram_bot\" (non-zero exit)']"], "metadata": {"budget_multiplier": 1.0, "original_task_id": "243fc900fa9b", "retry_mode": "cold", "retry_requested_mode": "warm", "retry_decision_hash": "6c8c13e631b099e8193eda079a7973fa25f3035eb0dd27312d96dbcaac5ee359", "retry_downgrade_reason": "no_checkpoint", "janitor_reopen_count": 2}, "max_turns": null}
 {"id": "494a42ae6a55", "title": "Extract startup notification logic out of app/__init__.py into app/core/startup.py", "description": "`app/__init__.py` is 228 lines because it inlines two big functions: `send_startup_notification` (async orchestrator) and `send_startup_notifications` (sync fan-out with duplicated Telegram loop). This violates the app-factory pattern and duplicates work already in `app/core/notifications.py`.\n\n## What to change\n1. Create NEW file `app/core/startup.py` and MOVE both functions there verbatim (preserve every string, log line, comment, and control flow). Fix imports so the moved code still resolves (`from .. import config`, `from ..db import get_db_connection`, `from .notifications import send_group_telegram_notification, send_ntfy_notification`).\n2. In `app/__init__.py`:\n   - Remove the two function bodies.\n   - Add `from .core.startup import send_startup_notification` near the other core imports.\n   - Leave `run_background_tasks` calling `send_startup_notification()` unchanged.\n   - Remove the now-unused imports (`import requests` inside the deleted body was local; safe to drop). Keep everything else \u2014 do NOT touch `create_app`, the Flask setup, CORS, or the `/` route.\n3. Do NOT deduplicate the inner Telegram loop against `send_telegram_notification` in this task \u2014 a follow-up task will do that once repositories exist. This task is a pure move + import fix so it stays reviewable.\n\n## Files you own\n`app/__init__.py` and NEW file `app/core/startup.py` \u2014 nothing else.\n\n## Acceptance\n- `app/core/startup.py` exists and defines `send_startup_notification` and `send_startup_notifications`.\n- `grep -n \"def send_startup_notification\" app/__init__.py` returns nothing.\n- `grep -n \"def send_startup_notifications\" app/__init__.py` returns nothing.\n- `wc -l app/__init__.py` shows <= 110 lines (down from 228).\n- `python -c \"from app import create_app, run_background_tasks; from app.core.startup import send_startup_notification\"` exits 0.", "role": "backend", "priority": 0, "scope": "medium", "complexity": "low", "estimated_minutes": 240, "status": "open", "task_type": "standard", "upgrade_details": null, "depends_on": [], "parent_task_id": null, "depends_on_repo": null, "owned_files": ["app/__init__.py", "app/core/startup.py"], "assigned_agent": null, "result_summary": null, "tenant_id": "default", "cell_id": null, "repo": null, "batch_eligible": false, "eu_ai_act_risk": "minimal", "approval_required": false, "risk_level": "low", "slack_context": null, "version": 7, "claimed_at": null, "completed_at": null, "closed_at": null, "claimed_by_session": null, "parent_session_id": null, "subtask_wait_started_at": null, "parent_context": null, "retry_count": 1, "max_retries": 3, "retry_delay_s": 0.0, "terminal_reason": null, "max_output_tokens": null, "meta_messages": ["Retry 1: Previous attempt failed with reason: Agent backend-c1a0df4d died; janitor failed: ['path_exists: app/core/startup.py (not found)', 'file_contains: app/core/startup.py :: def send_startup_notification (not found)', 'file_contains: app/core/startup.py :: def send_startup_notifications (not found)', 'test_passes: python -c \"from app import create_app, run_background_tasks; from app.core.startup import send_startup_notification\" (non-zero exit)']"], "metadata": {"budget_multiplier": 1.0, "original_task_id": "ef92d5f815ea", "retry_mode": "cold", "retry_requested_mode": "warm", "retry_decision_hash": "bac13288680154ef392a2ac85722cd9487de4871871782a21d9bc9e2ed2ff866", "retry_downgrade_reason": "no_checkpoint", "janitor_reopen_count": 2}, "max_turns": null}
 {"id": "c3849e1033a8", "title": "Consolidate flask-restx model registration and extract URL validators", "description": "Two related quality issues in the API layer:\n\n### 1. Triple-registered models\n`add_models_to_api` in `app/models.py` builds and returns a fresh dict of models every time it is called. It is currently called THREE times: once from `app/__init__.py` on the top-level `Api`, once from `app/api/endpoints.py` on its `Namespace`, once from `app/api/settings.py` on its `Namespace`. Each call registers a new model instance \u2014 the objects that `endpoints.py:models[\"endpoint\"]` and `__init__.py`s api models refer to are DIFFERENT objects. flask-restx tolerates this but it wastes work and makes the intent unclear.\n\n### 2. URL validation lives in the API layer\n`normalize_url` and `validate_url` are defined at the top of `app/api/endpoints.py`. They have no HTTP or Flask dependency and belong in a domain module so tests and future callers (e.g., a CLI) can reuse them.\n\n## What to change\n1. Refactor `app/models.py`:\n   - Split `add_models_to_api` into two functions:\n     - `build_models(api_or_ns)` \u2014 the existing body, unchanged (some flask-restx workflows need per-namespace registration for swagger discoverability, so preserve it as-is).\n     - Add a module-level cache: `_MODELS: dict | None = None` and `def get_models(api) -> dict:` that lazily calls `build_models(api)` ONCE and returns the cached dict on subsequent calls.\n   - Keep `add_models_to_api` as a thin alias for `build_models` for backwards compat with the two namespace files during migration.\n2. Update `app/api/endpoints.py`:\n   - Move `normalize_url` and `validate_url` (plus the inner `is_valid_ipv4`) to NEW file `app/core/validators.py`.\n   - Import them: `from ..core.validators import normalize_url, validate_url`.\n   - No behavior change; identical function bodies.\n3. Update `app/api/settings.py`: no logic change needed unless it breaks \u2014 leave as-is (keeps its `add_models_to_api(ns)` call for now).\n4. Create `app/core/validators.py` with the two functions (plus the inner helper made module-private as `_is_valid_ipv4`).\n\n## Files you own\n`app/models.py`, `app/api/endpoints.py`, `app/api/settings.py`, and NEW file `app/core/validators.py`. Nothing else. In particular, do NOT touch `app/__init__.py` (Task 5 owns it), `app/core/monitoring.py` (Task 3), `app/core/telegram_bot.py` (Task 4), `app/repositories/*` (Task 2), or `app/core/startup.py` (Task 5).\n\n## Acceptance\n- `app/core/validators.py` exists and exports `normalize_url`, `validate_url`.\n- `grep -n \"def normalize_url\" app/api/endpoints.py` returns nothing.\n- `grep -n \"def validate_url\" app/api/endpoints.py` returns nothing.\n- `grep -n \"def get_models\" app/models.py` returns 1 match.\n- `python -c \"from app.core.validators import normalize_url, validate_url; assert validate_url(\\\"example.com\\\"); assert not validate_url(\\\"not a url with spaces\\\"); print(normalize_url(\\\"example.com\\\"))\"` prints `https://example.com` and exits 0.", "role": "architect", "priority": 2, "scope": "medium", "complexity": "medium", "estimated_minutes": 120, "status": "done", "task_type": "standard", "upgrade_details": null, "depends_on": [], "parent_task_id": null, "depends_on_repo": null, "owned_files": ["app/models.py", "app/api/endpoints.py", "app/api/settings.py", "app/core/validators.py"], "assigned_agent": null, "result_summary": "Split app/models.py::add_models_to_api into build_models (per-namespace body preserved) + cached get_models(api); kept add_models_to_api as thin alias for backward compat. Extracted normalize_url and validate_url (with inner _is_valid_ipv4) from app/api/endpoints.py into new app/core/validators.py \u2014 identical bodies, no behavior change. endpoints.py now imports them from ..core.validators. All acceptance checks pass (validators file exists, no def normalize_url/validate_url left in endpoints.py, def get_models present in models.py, one-liner import test prints https://example.com).", "tenant_id": "default", "cell_id": null, "repo": null, "batch_eligible": false, "eu_ai_act_risk": "limited", "approval_required": false, "risk_level": "medium", "slack_context": null, "version": 3, "claimed_at": 1786297236.0471225, "completed_at": 1786297572.8283107, "closed_at": null, "claimed_by_session": "4163e54405e04cd9", "parent_session_id": null, "subtask_wait_started_at": null, "parent_context": null, "retry_count": 0, "max_retries": 3, "retry_delay_s": 0.0, "terminal_reason": null, "max_output_tokens": null, "meta_messages": [], "metadata": {}, "max_turns": null}
